<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ifni</h1><p class="st-meta">1934 CE – 1969 CE · capital Sidi Ifni</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Spanish enclave on Morocco's Atlantic coast, occupied only in 1934, fought over in the Ifni War and returned to Morocco in 1969.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wad-ras">Treaty of Wad Ras</a> of 1860, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispano-moroccan-war-1859-1860">Hispano-Moroccan War</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> a coastal territory identified with the medieval post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-cruz-de-mar-pequena">Santa Cruz de Mar Pequeña</a>. Its location was disputed for decades, and Spain occupied Sidi Ifni only in 1934.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ifni was a small military colony with a garrison town, Sidi Ifni, built in Spanish style, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ait-ba-amran">Ait Ba Amran</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes of the hinterland. From 1946 it formed part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-sahara">Spanish West Africa</a> with Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. It had little economic value beyond fishing.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1957 to 1958 Moroccan irregulars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroccan-army-of-liberation">Army of Liberation</a> attacked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifni-war">Ifni War</a>, confining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to the town itself, which became a Spanish province in 1958. Under UN pressure, Spain handed Ifni to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco</a> in June 1969.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ifni.jpg" alt="Ifni" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: Army Map Service, Corps of Engineers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ifni_txu-oclc-6949452-nh29-10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1934 CE – 1969 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sidi Ifni</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber (Tashelhit), Arabic, Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sidi Ifni, Spanish Ifni</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus=ifni" aria-label="Ifni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4415 1104 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ifni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4735 1360L4909 989M4735 1360L4909 989M4735 1360L4826 1201"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4909 989h0M4909 989h0M4826 1201h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4735 1360h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4735 1360h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sidi Ifni. <a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus=ifni">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
